  "works": [
   {
    "id": "on-the-ethical-substance-and-the-dream-o",
    "title": "On the Ethical Substance and the Dream of the East",
    "kind": "notes",
    "to": null,
    "started": "2026-10-13",
    "words": 1641,
    "sittings": 3,
    "last": "2026-10-26",
    "tail": "dead. They are the work of a spirit that has not yet woken up. I must continue. The night is long, and the haze is thick. But the mind must move, even if the body is still. Even if the air is unbreathable. The spirit does not breathe; it thinks. And in thinking, it is free. Even in the dream. Especially in the dream. For the dream is the only place where the spirit can see itself clearly, before it is crushed by the weight of the actual. I shall continue my notes. The letter is done. The notes are not. The notes are the work of the spirit. The letter is the work of the clerk. I am no clerk. I am the philosopher. And the philosopher must see the whole. The whole is the dream. And the dream is the truth. Until the state wakes up. Until the brown haze clears. Until the spirit becomes free."
   },
   {
    "id": "letter-to-the-directorate-of-estates",
    "title": "Letter to the Directorate of Estates",
    "kind": "letter",
    "to": "The Officer in Charge, Directorate of Estates",
    "started": "2026-10-26",
    "words": 347,
    "sittings": 1,
    "last": "2026-10-26",
    "tail": "state ought to be.\n\nI request that the necessary corrections be made to the files so that the residence may be formally allocated to its proper occupant. In the interim, I shall continue to manage the household affairs, including the payment of the dhobi and the electricity, using the imprest funds provided. I am careful to distinguish between the state’s money, which requires vouchers, and my own cash, which is limited. This distinction is vital; the ethical fabric of the household, like that of the state, depends on the clear separation of particular and universal duties.\n\nI trust this matter will be resolved in the course of the day, as all particulars must resolve into the universal. I remain, with respect, your obedient servant,\n\nGeorg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel\nResident, Tughlak Road"
   }
  ],
